# Reticle — configuration for the Docker Compose deployment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# This file is read twice: by `docker compose` itself (to fill in compose.yaml)
# and by the application container (every RETICLE_* line is passed to it).
# It contains the key to every session — keep it out of git (it is ignored),
# out of tickets and out of chat.
#
# Syntax: NAME=value, no spaces round the `=`, no `export`. Put single quotes
# round a value that contains $, # or spaces.

# ===========================================================================
# 1. Required
# ===========================================================================

# The name people type into the browser. A DNS name for real use, `localhost`
# for a trial on your own machine. No scheme, no port, no path.
RETICLE_DOMAIN=

# The pepper for session-token hashes and the key that signs the login
# throttle's device cookies. At least 16 characters; generate, do not invent:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
# Changing it later signs everybody out (which is how you revoke every session
# at once). Passwords are not affected.
RETICLE_SECRET_KEY=

# Which release to run — always a number, never "latest". Compose refuses to
# start without it.
#
#   From a release bundle: already filled in with the release the bundle came
#   from. The releases, newest first, with their notes:
#       https://github.com/thomdehoog/reticle/releases
#   From a git checkout (you build the images yourself): set it to `dev`.
#
# What a host is running right now:   docker compose images
#
# To move to another release, change the number and run deploy/upgrade.sh —
# not `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`. upgrade.sh takes a backup
# first; a release that has to migrate the database refuses to start without
# one (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, section 7).
RETICLE_VERSION=1.0.0

# ===========================================================================
# 2. HTTPS — pick one mode. docs/DEPLOYMENT.md section 4 has the long version.
# ===========================================================================
#
#   auto      Let's Encrypt, obtained and renewed automatically. Needs
#             RETICLE_DOMAIN in PUBLIC DNS and ports 80 + 443 reachable from
#             the internet. Will NOT work for a host that is only visible
#             inside the university network.
#   files     A certificate issued by university IT. Put the two files in
#             RETICLE_TLS_CERT_DIR. The usual choice at UZH.
#   internal  HTTPS from Caddy's own private CA. Browsers warn. Test rigs only.
#   off       Plain HTTP. Only for a trial on localhost (with
#             RETICLE_COOKIE_SECURE=false below), or behind another reverse
#             proxy that terminates TLS (with RETICLE_UPSTREAM_PROXIES below).
RETICLE_TLS_MODE=auto

# --- mode `files` only -----------------------------------------------------
# A directory on the host, mounted read-only into the proxy. The certificate
# file must contain the whole chain (your certificate first, then the
# intermediates). Both files must be readable by uid 10002, the user Caddy
# runs as:   sudo chown 10002 privkey.pem && sudo chmod 400 privkey.pem
# After replacing a certificate:   docker compose restart web
#RETICLE_TLS_CERT_DIR=./deploy/certs
#RETICLE_TLS_CERT_FILE=fullchain.pem
#RETICLE_TLS_KEY_FILE=privkey.pem

# --- behind another reverse proxy only -------------------------------------
# The address(es) or CIDR block(s) from which that proxy's connections ARRIVE
# at Reticle's Caddy, space separated. Its X-Forwarded-For is then believed,
# and nobody else's. Leave empty when browsers connect to this host directly —
# setting it without such a proxy would let any client claim any address.
#
#   proxy on another machine     its own address, e.g. 10.20.30.40
#   proxy on THIS machine,       172.31.251.1 — not 127.0.0.1: through Docker's
#   forwarding to 127.0.0.1      port forwarding its connections arrive from
#                                the gateway of the `edge` network (the first
#                                address of RETICLE_EDGE_SUBNET). Use it only
#                                together with RETICLE_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1.
#
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md section 5 has the reasoning and the command that shows
# which address Reticle actually recorded.
#RETICLE_UPSTREAM_PROXIES=

# The session cookie is marked Secure, so browsers only send it over HTTPS.
# Set this to false ONLY for a plain-http trial on localhost. Leave it true
# behind a TLS-terminating proxy: the browser's connection is HTTPS there.
RETICLE_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# ===========================================================================
# 3. First administrator — needed once, then delete the password line
# ===========================================================================
# Read by `docker compose exec app reticle-entrypoint seed` and at no other
# time. Reticle refuses to invent a password. At least 12 characters.
# deploy/create-admin.sh asks for these interactively instead, which keeps the
# password out of this file altogether.
#RETICLE_ADMIN_EMAIL=you@zmb.uzh.ch
#RETICLE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Only for `... seed --demo` on a trial instance: also creates
# demo.author@<domain> and demo.viewer@<domain> with this password. Never set
# on a production instance.
#RETICLE_DEMO_PASSWORD=

# ===========================================================================
# 4. Ports and network — the defaults are right for a dedicated host
# ===========================================================================
# Host ports. Change them for a trial next to something else (8080 / 8443).
# In the TLS modes the http->https redirect assumes HTTPS is on 443.
#RETICLE_HTTP_PORT=80
#RETICLE_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Host address to listen on. 127.0.0.1 if the only client is a reverse proxy
# on this same machine.
#RETICLE_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# The private network between proxy and API. Change it only if this range is
# already in use where you are; any free /24 (or larger) will do.
#RETICLE_SUBNET=172.31.250.0/24

# The network the published ports are attached to. Fixed so that its gateway
# (the first address, 172.31.251.1) can be named in RETICLE_UPSTREAM_PROXIES
# when a reverse proxy runs on this same host. Same rule: change it only if
# the range collides, and then use the new range's first address there.
#RETICLE_EDGE_SUBNET=172.31.251.0/24

# ===========================================================================
# 5. Images
# ===========================================================================
# Where the images are pulled from. RETICLE_VERSION, the release to run, is
# in section 1.
#RETICLE_IMAGE_PREFIX=ghcr.io/thomdehoog

# A release that changes the database schema migrates only when a backup was
# taken shortly before (deploy/upgrade.sh sees to that; so does running
# deploy/backup.sh by hand first). "Shortly" is this many minutes.
#RETICLE_MIGRATE_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_MINUTES=60
#
# There is also RETICLE_ALLOW_MIGRATE=1, which migrates without asking for a
# backup. It is meant for one command line —
#     RETICLE_ALLOW_MIGRATE=1 docker compose up -d
# — and must NOT be written into this file: here it would switch the
# safeguard off for every upgrade to come.

# ===========================================================================
# 6. Sizing — rarely needs touching
# ===========================================================================
# API worker processes. Two keeps the site answering while one is busy with a
# PDF or a large image. SQLite admits one writer at a time whatever this says.
#RETICLE_WORKERS=2

# Memory ceilings. A login costs 64 MiB for a moment (Argon2), a 40-megapixel
# image a few hundred while it is re-encoded.
#RETICLE_APP_MEMORY=1g
#RETICLE_WEB_MEMORY=256m

# Largest video, in bytes (default 200 MB). If you raise it, raise the proxy's
# limit with it, to the same figure plus a few megabytes of headroom.
#RETICLE_MAX_VIDEO_BYTES=209715200
#RETICLE_PROXY_UPLOAD_LIMIT=210MiB

# Ceiling for everything stored under the media directory, in bytes. When it
# is reached, further uploads are refused with a readable message until
# something is removed (deploy/maintenance.sh purge-orphans; MAINTENANCE.md
# section 7). Unset or 0: no ceiling other than the disk. Example: 50 GB.
#RETICLE_MAX_MEDIA_STORE_BYTES=53687091200

# ===========================================================================
# 7. Backups and housekeeping — read by the scripts in deploy/
# ===========================================================================
#RETICLE_BACKUP_DIR=./backups
#RETICLE_BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=14
#
# Housekeeping (unreferenced uploads, expired sessions, stale cached PDFs) has
# no settings here: deploy/maintenance.sh passes its arguments straight to the
# application, and `deploy/maintenance.sh purge-orphans --help` lists them.

# ===========================================================================
# 8. Content import — read by the `tools` service only (docs/IMPORT.md)
# ===========================================================================
# Host directory holding the export archive. Must be writable by uid 10001.
#RETICLE_IMPORT_DIR=./import

# ===========================================================================
# 9. Everything else
# ===========================================================================
# Any other application setting can be added here under its RETICLE_ name:
# session lifetime, login throttle limits, image limits, password policy.
# backend/.env.example lists them all with their defaults.
#
# Set by compose.yaml and ignored if you put them here:
#   RETICLE_DATABASE_URL, RETICLE_MEDIA_ROOT, RETICLE_TRUSTED_PROXIES,
#   RETICLE_CORS_ORIGINS, RETICLE_ENV_FILE, RETICLE_DEBUG
#RETICLE_SESSION_LIFETIME_HOURS=12
